103
00:05:57,650 --> 00:06:01,560
OK, so with that, I first want
to start with post-training

104
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:04,880
because a lot of
our efforts on how

105
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:09,920
to make these models able to
reason starts from post-training

106
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:12,180
or happens during
the post-training.

107
00:06:12,180 --> 00:06:15,440
And I talk-- we'll talk
about how we develop

108
00:06:15,440 --> 00:06:19,145
Tulu along this process.

109
00:06:19,145 --> 00:06:22,830
So how do we build a modern
large language model.

110
00:06:22,830 --> 00:06:26,150
So it is roughly going
these two stages,

111
00:06:26,150 --> 00:06:28,940
pretraining and post-training.

112
00:06:28,940 --> 00:06:32,210
In pretraining, the
models are trained

113
00:06:32,210 --> 00:06:37,550
with predicting the next
token where data usually

114
00:06:37,550 --> 00:06:43,280
comes from long and large scale
data, mostly from the web.

115
00:06:43,280 --> 00:06:47,870
But the model that
comes out of this stage

116
00:06:47,870 --> 00:06:51,640
is not ready to be used
for a lot of applications.

117
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:52,600
It is not safe.

118
00:06:52,600 --> 00:06:55,960
It's probably not able to
follow human instructions.

119
00:06:55,960 --> 00:07:00,870
And it's not that
good at reasoning

120
00:07:00,870 --> 00:07:04,870
When post-training happens, now
models can do a lot of things.

121
00:07:04,870 --> 00:07:05,710
It can chat.

122
00:07:05,710 --> 00:07:07,210
It follows instructions.

123
00:07:07,210 --> 00:07:09,220
You can integrate
it with tool use.

124
00:07:09,220 --> 00:07:10,920
It's able to reason.

125
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:15,750
It's able to tell you when it is
harmful when it doesn't follow

126
00:07:15,750 --> 00:07:18,460
the instruction, and so on.

127
00:07:18,460 --> 00:07:22,930
So we do post-training to
align with human preferences.

128
00:07:22,930 --> 00:07:25,220
You've seen it when,
for example, you

129
00:07:25,220 --> 00:07:28,570
use ChatGPT, which of these
two responses you prefer.

130
00:07:28,570 --> 00:07:31,020
And we collect these
type of data to say,

131
00:07:31,020 --> 00:07:34,660
which of these outcomes are
more aligned with humans?

132
00:07:34,660 --> 00:07:39,420
And we do post-training
to be able to follow,

133
00:07:39,420 --> 00:07:43,150
to do-- to use a searching
code execution and so on.

134
00:07:43,150 --> 00:07:47,460
And also we do a lot in
post-training for models

135
00:07:47,460 --> 00:07:48,670
to be able to reason.

136
00:07:48,670 --> 00:07:50,910
For example, in
this case, we want

137
00:07:50,910 --> 00:07:56,730
to teach the model how to solve
this arithmetic word problem.

138
00:07:56,730 --> 00:07:59,520
OK, so how does it work?

139
00:07:59,520 --> 00:08:04,290
Following many standard language
modeling or machine learning

140
00:08:04,290 --> 00:08:08,800
pipelines, it
requires these pieces,

141
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:11,250
the data that we
need to train on,

142
00:08:11,250 --> 00:08:13,890
a training
architecture and neural

143
00:08:13,890 --> 00:08:15,850
network, which in
almost all cases

144
00:08:15,850 --> 00:08:18,000
it's a transformer-based
architecture,

145
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,840
and a very thorough
evaluation loop

146
00:08:21,840 --> 00:08:23,675
to tell us where
we are improving

147
00:08:23,675 --> 00:08:26,872
and where things are
not going so well.

148
00:08:26,872 --> 00:08:28,740
And I'm going to
talk about these

149
00:08:28,740 --> 00:08:31,770
are the 3 integral
parts in building

150
00:08:31,770 --> 00:08:35,880
a very modern successful
language model data, models,

151
00:08:35,880 --> 00:08:37,179
and algorithms.

152
00:08:37,179 --> 00:08:41,580
And during this talk, I'm going
to talk about, what type of data

153
00:08:41,580 --> 00:08:43,950
is important and what
type of algorithms

154
00:08:43,950 --> 00:08:47,740
are useful for
which type of data?

155
00:08:47,740 --> 00:08:50,880
So data plays a
really important role

156
00:08:50,880 --> 00:08:55,150
in building these post-train
models and pre-trained model.

157
00:08:55,150 --> 00:08:59,500
It comes from different sources
in many different forms.

158
00:08:59,500 --> 00:09:03,880
And they are targeting
diverse capabilities.

159
00:09:03,880 --> 00:09:06,570
So for example we need data
to be able to solve math word

160
00:09:06,570 --> 00:09:07,390
problems.

161
00:09:07,390 --> 00:09:09,370
We need data to build agents.

162
00:09:09,370 --> 00:09:12,480
We need data to tell
the model when it is not

163
00:09:12,480 --> 00:09:15,750
safe to respond to a query.

164
00:09:15,750 --> 00:09:20,760
But we need to design and
use different algorithms

165
00:09:20,760 --> 00:09:24,640
on deciding when to use
the right data and how--

166
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:27,880
which of these algorithms
are useful when.

167
00:09:27,880 --> 00:09:32,350
So this was our effort
throughout this Tulu project,

168
00:09:32,350 --> 00:09:35,250
which started from 2023.

169
00:09:35,250 --> 00:09:39,360
And now we have an open,
reproducible, and state

170
00:09:39,360 --> 00:09:41,430
of the art post-training recipe.

171
00:09:41,430 --> 00:09:45,970
And we can apply it on many
different open rate models.

172
00:09:45,970 --> 00:09:51,310
In the first version, which
we worked on June 2023,

173
00:09:51,310 --> 00:09:54,570
we pretty much only
showed, what is the best

174
00:09:54,570 --> 00:09:59,130
recipe to develop supervised
finetuning data or instruction

175
00:09:59,130 --> 00:09:59,650
data?

176
00:09:59,650 --> 00:10:02,100
I'm going to dig into all
these a little bit deeper

177
00:10:02,100 --> 00:10:03,870
in the next slides.

178
00:10:03,870 --> 00:10:08,790
In the second version, we showed
that how we bring and digest

179
00:10:08,790 --> 00:10:11,700
a preference data.

180
00:10:11,700 --> 00:10:15,990
And then finally, we started
doing very systematic study on,

181
00:10:15,990 --> 00:10:19,800
what are the best algorithms
to use these type of preference

182
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:20,590
tuning data?

183
00:10:20,590 --> 00:10:22,440
And at the end of
these stages, we

184
00:10:22,440 --> 00:10:26,440
managed to show that with
only open source models,

185
00:10:26,440 --> 00:10:32,250
we can be as good as ChatGPT,
even upsetting GPT-4.

186
00:10:32,250 --> 00:10:36,490
Finally, at the end of
last year in November,

187
00:10:36,490 --> 00:10:40,380
we released a Tulu 3, which
pretty much put all our efforts

188
00:10:40,380 --> 00:10:43,680
together such that
we develop Tulu 3.

189
00:10:43,680 --> 00:10:46,560
And then we applied
the same recipe on OLMo

190
00:10:46,560 --> 00:10:51,486
showing that the fully open
training recipe also works.

191
00:10:51,486 --> 00:10:53,620
So what is this training recipe?

192
00:10:53,620 --> 00:10:56,790
So it starts from
a base model and we

193
00:10:56,790 --> 00:11:00,670
are following three important
steps, instruction tuning,

194
00:11:00,670 --> 00:11:04,630
preference tuning, and
our new novel method,

195
00:11:04,630 --> 00:11:08,642
we call it reinforcement
learning with verifiable reward.

196
00:11:08,642 --> 00:11:11,940
And sometimes we have to do
a bunch of back and forth

197
00:11:11,940 --> 00:11:13,590
between these
stages and I'm going

198
00:11:13,590 --> 00:11:15,480
to talk about how this works.

199
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:20,350
OK, so let's first start getting
the ingredients to start with,

200
00:11:20,350 --> 00:11:21,400
which is data.

201
00:11:21,400 --> 00:11:25,590
So in order to do a
successful post-training,

202
00:11:25,590 --> 00:11:29,670
we first need to establish
meaningful evaluations

203
00:11:29,670 --> 00:11:31,480
for targeted skills.

204
00:11:31,480 --> 00:11:34,600
For example, for Tulu
3 these were our goal.

205
00:11:34,600 --> 00:11:37,690
We want to have a general
chat capabilities.

206
00:11:37,690 --> 00:11:39,810
We want our model
to be able to do

207
00:11:39,810 --> 00:11:44,460
to be good at knowledge, recall,
math, and reasoning, coding,

208
00:11:44,460 --> 00:11:48,300
and safety and non-compliance,
multilingual, and also

209
00:11:48,300 --> 00:11:52,290
very precisely follow
instructions even when

210
00:11:52,290 --> 00:11:56,380
it shows some constraints.

211
00:11:56,380 --> 00:12:00,090
So we developed all these
evaluation benchmarks,

212
00:12:00,090 --> 00:12:03,730
we decided, what type
of evaluation data set?

213
00:12:03,730 --> 00:12:06,600
Sometimes we also had to
create the data sets ourselves

214
00:12:06,600 --> 00:12:10,170
on how to evaluate if
our model has acquired

215
00:12:10,170 --> 00:12:14,040
good capabilities in
all of these categories

216
00:12:14,040 --> 00:12:15,755
that I just talked about.

217
00:12:15,755 --> 00:12:19,360
And we also followed really good
old machine learning techniques,

218
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:22,980
which we set some of these
data sets for development

219
00:12:22,980 --> 00:12:25,450
and then kept some as hidden.

220
00:12:25,450 --> 00:12:28,230
And we just evaluated
at the end of-- when

221
00:12:28,230 --> 00:12:30,885
all our training was done.

222
00:12:30,885 --> 00:12:34,450
So given our emphasis
on these capabilities,

223
00:12:34,450 --> 00:12:38,970
we started collecting prompts
like queries and instructions

224
00:12:38,970 --> 00:12:42,360
that we think our model
should be able to answer.

225
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:44,230
We checked for licenses.

226
00:12:44,230 --> 00:12:49,080
Are they collected
with a user consent?

227
00:12:49,080 --> 00:12:51,100
How the licensing work.

228
00:12:51,100 --> 00:12:55,050
And then make sure that
we decontaminate them

229
00:12:55,050 --> 00:12:58,660
with the evaluation sets
that we are evaluating on.

230
00:12:58,660 --> 00:13:00,780
This is very important
because we've

231
00:13:00,780 --> 00:13:04,050
noticed in the literature people
show some really good data sets

232
00:13:04,050 --> 00:13:07,000
getting really good results
on certain benchmarks.

233
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:10,470
But the decontamination
wasn't done carefully

234
00:13:10,470 --> 00:13:14,230
because we are talking
about very complex instances

235
00:13:14,230 --> 00:13:17,280
and it's very easy
for some of the test

236
00:13:17,280 --> 00:13:19,690
sets to be different--
to be contaminated.

237
00:13:19,690 --> 00:13:23,040
So in our open
instruct toolkit, we

238
00:13:23,040 --> 00:13:26,650
have a code available
for all these cases.

239
00:13:26,650 --> 00:13:29,850
And it's really
useful to follow this.

240
00:13:29,850 --> 00:13:32,700
OK, so now that we
have the ingredients,

241
00:13:32,700 --> 00:13:34,800
we want to go through
the first step, which

242
00:13:34,800 --> 00:13:37,560
is instruction tuning
or sometimes called

243
00:13:37,560 --> 00:13:39,330
supervised finetuning.

244
00:13:39,330 --> 00:13:41,160
What does that mean?

245
00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:43,960
It basically is a
very simple step.

246
00:13:43,960 --> 00:13:48,340
It takes a lot of these
prompts and completions

247
00:13:48,340 --> 00:13:50,970
and then fine tune a
pre-trained language

248
00:13:50,970 --> 00:13:53,680
model to be able to
follow these instructions.

249
00:13:53,680 --> 00:13:57,120
So for example, we can
have a queries like answer

250
00:13:57,120 --> 00:13:59,100
these questions one by one.

251
00:13:59,100 --> 00:14:03,580
Summarize this talk like many
of these type of questions

252
00:14:03,580 --> 00:14:05,962
and then the final answers.

253
00:14:05,962 --> 00:14:08,230
But how do we curate this data?

254
00:14:08,230 --> 00:14:10,020
How do we source this data?

255
00:14:10,020 --> 00:14:15,060
We can have and source this data
through human annotation, which

256
00:14:15,060 --> 00:14:18,190
obviously it is costly,
it is time-consuming,

257
00:14:18,190 --> 00:14:20,440
and also it has high
variance right now.

258
00:14:20,440 --> 00:14:23,590
Sometimes, particularly
in tasks like reasoning,

259
00:14:23,590 --> 00:14:26,200
we are dealing with
complex annotations.

260
00:14:26,200 --> 00:14:31,830
And it might not be well
suited for general annotators.

261
00:14:31,830 --> 00:14:37,410
Another approach, which we
introduced in our 2023 self

262
00:14:37,410 --> 00:14:41,520
and strike paper was to
synthetically generate

263
00:14:41,520 --> 00:14:45,930
these data with self-loop
or self-instruction loop

264
00:14:45,930 --> 00:14:48,330
with another language model.

265
00:14:48,330 --> 00:14:50,580
So synthetic data generation.

266
00:14:50,580 --> 00:14:52,630
And obviously, it is good.

267
00:14:52,630 --> 00:14:54,400
It's really good
in a lot of cases,

268
00:14:54,400 --> 00:14:56,860
but it's going to
be noisy as well OK,

269
00:14:56,860 --> 00:14:59,070
and it is biased to
the language model

270
00:14:59,070 --> 00:15:01,690
that we are generating
this data from.

271
00:15:01,690 --> 00:15:05,460
And most recently, we've
discovered the optimal solution

272
00:15:05,460 --> 00:15:09,570
is combining both of
them, which we build

273
00:15:09,570 --> 00:15:13,740
a hybrid collection of data
coming from human annotation

274
00:15:13,740 --> 00:15:17,760
and also from synthetically
generated by other language

275
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:19,590
models.

276
00:15:19,590 --> 00:15:24,120
So data curation has
been received well

277
00:15:24,120 --> 00:15:26,850
within the open
source community.

278
00:15:26,850 --> 00:15:31,600
We had some earlier
work in 2022 on,

279
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:34,170
we called it natural
instructions and supernatural

280
00:15:34,170 --> 00:15:39,190
instructions where we only
focused on NLP relevant tasks

281
00:15:39,190 --> 00:15:41,610
where we were looking
at tasks-like question

282
00:15:41,610 --> 00:15:44,470
answering, summarization,
translation, and so on.

283
00:15:44,470 --> 00:15:47,490
Then we developed the
self-instruct, a framework where

284
00:15:47,490 --> 00:15:49,720
we generate data synthetically.

285
00:15:49,720 --> 00:15:53,080
This has picked up really
well within the community.

286
00:15:53,080 --> 00:15:58,110
Many next efforts have been
introduced within the community

287
00:15:58,110 --> 00:16:01,960
to generate more and more
synthetic data sets like Alpaca,

288
00:16:01,960 --> 00:16:05,640
Vicuna, and so on and so forth.

289
00:16:05,640 --> 00:16:09,060
We also have a very
good collection

290
00:16:09,060 --> 00:16:14,940
of data sets that are mostly
focused on short form question

291
00:16:14,940 --> 00:16:18,190
answering type tasks,
like for MV1 and so on.

292
00:16:18,190 --> 00:16:22,080
So I'm just listing a few
of these data sets here.

293
00:16:22,080 --> 00:16:26,500
For the full set, please
check out Tulu 3 paper.

294
00:16:26,500 --> 00:16:29,940
We listed a lot of
publicly available data

295
00:16:29,940 --> 00:16:32,490
sets, either manually
curated, expert

296
00:16:32,490 --> 00:16:36,520
curated, or synthetically
generated wider community.

297
00:16:36,520 --> 00:16:38,830
And we combine a lot of them.

298
00:16:38,830 --> 00:16:42,180
Use the ones that we saw
most signal out of them in

299
00:16:42,180 --> 00:16:45,780
to build our final SFT mix.

300
00:16:45,780 --> 00:16:52,630
OK, so now we have looked at all
these data that are available.

301
00:16:52,630 --> 00:16:56,280
And now, we are doing these two
repeated parallelizable tracks

302
00:16:56,280 --> 00:16:58,360
where we collect this data.

303
00:16:58,360 --> 00:17:01,500
And now we want to
mix them such that we

304
00:17:01,500 --> 00:17:05,770
get high accuracy in the
capabilities that we care about.

305
00:17:05,770 --> 00:17:08,260
This requires
substantial effort.

306
00:17:08,260 --> 00:17:08,760
Why?

307
00:17:08,760 --> 00:17:11,550
Because, for example,
if I add a lot

308
00:17:11,550 --> 00:17:15,930
of data about form generation,
probably my math reasoning

309
00:17:15,930 --> 00:17:17,470
capabilities drop.

310
00:17:17,470 --> 00:17:20,290
If I add a lot of data
on math reasoning,

311
00:17:20,290 --> 00:17:23,405
probably I would lose
this creative task

312
00:17:23,405 --> 00:17:25,000
like poem generation.

313
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,720
So it's a pretty
challenging task

314
00:17:27,720 --> 00:17:31,260
to keep a good balance
of all these data

315
00:17:31,260 --> 00:17:36,410
such that we are getting high
accuracy across the board.

316
00:17:36,410 --> 00:17:39,970
So we are exploring and
also in the community

317
00:17:39,970 --> 00:17:42,480
people are exploring
many different ways

318
00:17:42,480 --> 00:17:49,080
of this data mixing including
manual empirical analysis,

319
00:17:49,080 --> 00:17:51,780
automated mixing.

320
00:17:51,780 --> 00:17:55,410
And I think the
literature is evolving

321
00:17:55,410 --> 00:17:59,670
and there are a lot of active
research going on in this area.

322
00:17:59,670 --> 00:18:03,330
OK, so let me show you
some of our efforts

323
00:18:03,330 --> 00:18:07,440
and how we did this data
mixing in our Tulu 1,

324
00:18:07,440 --> 00:18:09,190
which was very simple.

325
00:18:09,190 --> 00:18:13,890
There were not much
data available in 2023

326
00:18:13,890 --> 00:18:17,010
such that we start doing
all these combinations.

327
00:18:17,010 --> 00:18:21,450
So first, we combined all
available open data sets

328
00:18:21,450 --> 00:18:23,830
that were available back then.

329
00:18:23,830 --> 00:18:27,060
And I'm listing
them on the left.

330
00:18:27,060 --> 00:18:31,470
And some of them are created
by humans, general public,

331
00:18:31,470 --> 00:18:33,490
or experts.

332
00:18:33,490 --> 00:18:37,440
And then some are synthesized
with some of the most successful

333
00:18:37,440 --> 00:18:39,970
language models back then.

334
00:18:39,970 --> 00:18:42,850
Then, similar to what
I just described,

335
00:18:42,850 --> 00:18:48,330
we started looking at
evaluation sets or data

336
00:18:48,330 --> 00:18:51,910
sets targeted to evaluate
certain capabilities.

337
00:18:51,910 --> 00:18:55,920
For example, this is the
outcome of our finetuning

338
00:18:55,920 --> 00:19:01,422
when we were evaluating on
chat type evaluation sets.

339
00:19:01,422 --> 00:19:04,270
And here are how
the results compare.

340
00:19:04,270 --> 00:19:07,330
The red ones mean the
results are not that good.

341
00:19:07,330 --> 00:19:11,550
And then the blue ones show,
OK, successful results.

342
00:19:11,550 --> 00:19:13,890
As you see here,
that GPT4-Alpaca

343
00:19:13,890 --> 00:19:17,620
shows good high results
on chat type evaluations.

344
00:19:17,620 --> 00:19:21,840
ShareGPT also showing good
results on chat evaluation.

345
00:19:21,840 --> 00:19:25,350
So we did that across
many different verticals

346
00:19:25,350 --> 00:19:28,330
knowledge, reasoning, coding,
multilinguality, and safety.

347
00:19:28,330 --> 00:19:30,750
And some of these results
are quite exciting.

348
00:19:30,750 --> 00:19:33,490
For example, when we train
with chain-of-thought data,

349
00:19:33,490 --> 00:19:39,120
we see higher numbers on
reasoning type evaluation sets.

350
00:19:39,120 --> 00:19:43,120
OK, so now the question
is, how do we combine them?

351
00:19:43,120 --> 00:19:46,330
How do we take the best
of all of these worlds?

352
00:19:46,330 --> 00:19:48,840
Because as you saw,
this data-- actually

353
00:19:48,840 --> 00:19:53,160
let me go back here--
this data like shared

354
00:19:53,160 --> 00:19:58,560
GPT, or self-instruct,
or like I would say,

355
00:19:58,560 --> 00:20:01,360
open the system one
that is good in chat,

356
00:20:01,360 --> 00:20:03,590
they are not that good in
knowledge or reasoning type

357
00:20:03,590 --> 00:20:04,090
test.

358
00:20:04,090 --> 00:20:07,680
So it is important to get
the best of all these worlds.

359
00:20:07,680 --> 00:20:10,770
So we try to look
at the data sets

360
00:20:10,770 --> 00:20:14,650
that show high numbers
across one of these verticals

361
00:20:14,650 --> 00:20:16,000
and I started combining them.

362
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:21,240
And as a result, we have a
human plus synthetic type data.

363
00:20:21,240 --> 00:20:24,510
I want to highlight that we saw
really good results with shared

364
00:20:24,510 --> 00:20:27,850
GPT early on, but we
later dropped this data

365
00:20:27,850 --> 00:20:32,260
because license and the
way the data was collected

366
00:20:32,260 --> 00:20:33,560
was not that good.

367
00:20:33,560 --> 00:20:37,420
So we dropped this data
in our next versions.

368
00:20:37,420 --> 00:20:41,480
OK, now with the
emphasis on reasoning,

369
00:20:41,480 --> 00:20:45,050
I want to dig a little bit
deeper on, what type of data,

370
00:20:45,050 --> 00:20:49,250
particularly is
useful for reasoning?

371
00:20:49,250 --> 00:20:50,450
Why is this challenging?

372
00:20:50,450 --> 00:20:52,250
Let me give you a math example.

373
00:20:52,250 --> 00:20:53,870
We want to solve this problem.

374
00:20:53,870 --> 00:20:56,510
The question is you
can read this here.

375
00:20:56,510 --> 00:20:59,420
A store has buy 2 get
1 free deal on shirts.

376
00:20:59,420 --> 00:21:01,310
Each shirt costs $25.

377
00:21:01,310 --> 00:21:05,510
If Sarah wants to buy 7 shirts,
how much will she spend?

378
00:21:05,510 --> 00:21:08,870
So one quick thing could
be, OK, I have this problem.

379
00:21:08,870 --> 00:21:11,390
The final answer is $125.

380
00:21:11,390 --> 00:21:14,900
It's pretty easy to come
up with that number.

381
00:21:14,900 --> 00:21:18,670
However, if I just
fine tune a model

382
00:21:18,670 --> 00:21:21,310
with this input-output
setting, probably I

383
00:21:21,310 --> 00:21:25,560
won't get that much out
of this training instance.

384
00:21:25,560 --> 00:21:28,270
So we all observe
in the community

385
00:21:28,270 --> 00:21:33,250
that it's really better if we
start integrating and using

386
00:21:33,250 --> 00:21:35,270
this type think
chain-of-thought output

387
00:21:35,270 --> 00:21:39,580
where we say we're going to
break this down into, OK, step

388
00:21:39,580 --> 00:21:43,180
one, do this, step two, do
this, and then break this

389
00:21:43,180 --> 00:21:45,560
down as if you are
explaining it to a child

390
00:21:45,560 --> 00:21:48,940
how to follow these steps to
come up with that final number

391
00:21:48,940 --> 00:21:51,220
$125.

392
00:21:51,220 --> 00:21:54,760
So why do to summarize,
why do we think

393
00:21:54,760 --> 00:21:57,350
chain-of-thought data is
helpful for reasoning.

394
00:21:57,350 --> 00:22:03,670
It's helping the model to handle
complex multi-step problems much

395
00:22:03,670 --> 00:22:04,780
easier.

396
00:22:04,780 --> 00:22:09,350
It also starts revealing the
model's reasoning process.

397
00:22:09,350 --> 00:22:13,030
Also, one thought is
a lot of these models

398
00:22:13,030 --> 00:22:16,420
during pretraining have
seen a lot of this type

399
00:22:16,420 --> 00:22:18,860
think chain-of-thought data.

400
00:22:18,860 --> 00:22:22,240
And then this starts
to boost them up,

401
00:22:22,240 --> 00:22:25,580
to boost this capability
up of saying, OK, remember,

402
00:22:25,580 --> 00:22:28,040
you knew how to
think step by step.

403
00:22:28,040 --> 00:22:32,290
Bring them up when you are
looking at these more complex

404
00:22:32,290 --> 00:22:34,600
math reasoning problems.

405
00:22:34,600 --> 00:22:38,440
Also, when we look at
these type of annotations.

406
00:22:38,440 --> 00:22:41,110
These type think
chain-of-thought data makes

407
00:22:41,110 --> 00:22:44,450
it much easier to spot
errors in the logic

408
00:22:44,450 --> 00:22:49,720
and also it resembles how we
are explaining things to humans

409
00:22:49,720 --> 00:22:52,930
or how humans
thought process work.

410
00:22:52,930 --> 00:22:55,540
OK, these are all
very good points

411
00:22:55,540 --> 00:22:58,480
about why this type think
chain-of-thought data is

412
00:22:58,480 --> 00:22:59,470
important.

413
00:22:59,470 --> 00:23:05,500
However, it's becoming more
and more challenging to observe

414
00:23:05,500 --> 00:23:09,970
and obtain manual annotation
for this type think

415
00:23:09,970 --> 00:23:11,330
chain-of-thought data.

416
00:23:11,330 --> 00:23:13,090
It is time consuming.

417
00:23:13,090 --> 00:23:15,670
It is very expensive.

418
00:23:15,670 --> 00:23:19,040
and it often requires
expert annotation.

419
00:23:19,040 --> 00:23:23,210
The example that I showed in
the last slide was quite easy,

420
00:23:23,210 --> 00:23:26,150
but think of more
challenging math problems.

421
00:23:26,150 --> 00:23:28,600
We need experts
to annotate those.

422
00:23:28,600 --> 00:23:31,760
And obviously, it is
difficult to scale.

423
00:23:31,760 --> 00:23:34,070
And also it is not
diverse enough.

424
00:23:34,070 --> 00:23:36,970
It is very hard
to boost diversity

425
00:23:36,970 --> 00:23:39,640
with all these
limitations that we have

426
00:23:39,640 --> 00:23:41,590
when we want to
collect this type think

427
00:23:41,590 --> 00:23:43,840
chain-of-thought data.

428
00:23:43,840 --> 00:23:47,500
So again, our approach
to address this solution

429
00:23:47,500 --> 00:23:51,160
was hybrid data
curation where we

430
00:23:51,160 --> 00:23:54,820
look at data mixing
and also selecting

431
00:23:54,820 --> 00:23:56,780
from existing resources.

432
00:23:56,780 --> 00:23:59,020
We also did some very--

433
00:23:59,020 --> 00:24:03,500
observed a very interesting
paper from the literature

434
00:24:03,500 --> 00:24:08,470
where they proposed to
use different personas

435
00:24:08,470 --> 00:24:11,540
to generate diverse data.

436
00:24:11,540 --> 00:24:14,360
So I'm referring
to this paper here,

437
00:24:14,360 --> 00:24:19,780
but the idea is we're going
to look at different skills,

438
00:24:19,780 --> 00:24:24,140
for example, math coding,
precisely follow instruction.

439
00:24:24,140 --> 00:24:27,860
But then we devise
different personas,

440
00:24:27,860 --> 00:24:31,720
and then we ask the
model to generate data

441
00:24:31,720 --> 00:24:36,560
for those skills, given the
persona that it is looking at.

442
00:24:36,560 --> 00:24:42,530
This would ensure high diversity
and enables us to scale further.

443
00:24:42,530 --> 00:24:44,290
What does that mean?

444
00:24:44,290 --> 00:24:47,600
So we develop this prompt.

445
00:24:47,600 --> 00:24:52,150
We say we want to create this
data with this specific persona

446
00:24:52,150 --> 00:24:52,870
in mind.

447
00:24:52,870 --> 00:24:55,780
For example, we want to
create a math problem

448
00:24:55,780 --> 00:24:59,950
given that we are creating
it for a chemical kinetics

449
00:24:59,950 --> 00:25:01,722
researcher.

450
00:25:01,722 --> 00:25:03,800
And now the problem
looks like this.

451
00:25:03,800 --> 00:25:08,320
Dr. Smith, a chemist, is
studying this and so on.

452
00:25:08,320 --> 00:25:11,110
Or we say we want to solve
this logical reasoning

453
00:25:11,110 --> 00:25:13,550
problem, again,
for that persona,

454
00:25:13,550 --> 00:25:15,770
for that chemical
kinetics researcher.

455
00:25:15,770 --> 00:25:19,462
We tried many
different personas.

456
00:25:19,462 --> 00:25:23,806
We designed this
type of math problems

457
00:25:23,806 --> 00:25:29,350
for experts, for children,
six-year-old children,

458
00:25:29,350 --> 00:25:32,450
or a computer
scientist, and musician.

459
00:25:32,450 --> 00:25:36,080
So you give many different
personas to the model,

460
00:25:36,080 --> 00:25:39,250
and then we ask the model
to generate it specifically

461
00:25:39,250 --> 00:25:41,800
designed for that persona.

462
00:25:41,800 --> 00:25:49,090
With that, we created more than
150k hard math problems and 50k

463
00:25:49,090 --> 00:25:51,160
grade school math problem.

464
00:25:51,160 --> 00:25:58,090
We created 35k Python coding and
precise instruction following.

465
00:25:58,090 --> 00:26:01,630
Then these were mostly
for generating prompts

466
00:26:01,630 --> 00:26:06,400
because this was the point
of boosting diversity

467
00:26:06,400 --> 00:26:09,250
for the types of problems
that we are getting.

468
00:26:09,250 --> 00:26:15,130
Then we used a combination
of GPT-4o and Claude Sonnet

469
00:26:15,130 --> 00:26:18,110
to generate a step
by step solutions,

470
00:26:18,110 --> 00:26:21,070
for example, for a given
math problem, a coding

471
00:26:21,070 --> 00:26:24,250
or precise
instruction following.

472
00:26:24,250 --> 00:26:27,340
OK, so now let's
look at the result.

473
00:26:27,340 --> 00:26:29,540
How successful and
useful these data are?

474
00:26:29,540 --> 00:26:34,150
OK, so let me walk through
how we read these figures.

475
00:26:34,150 --> 00:26:41,020
So in the figures below, you
are seeing results on two data

476
00:26:41,020 --> 00:26:49,270
sets math and GSMK8, which are
due to popular math data sets.

477
00:26:49,270 --> 00:26:54,160
Then each of these
bars show a combination

478
00:26:54,160 --> 00:26:57,370
of using our public data
sets, like the data sets

479
00:26:57,370 --> 00:27:00,970
that are available, for example,
for math or general purpose,

480
00:27:00,970 --> 00:27:05,500
combined with this
persona-driven synthetic math

481
00:27:05,500 --> 00:27:06,930
problems.

482
00:27:06,930 --> 00:27:11,920
And we varied the amount of
these persona-driven math data

483
00:27:11,920 --> 00:27:13,400
in our experiments.

484
00:27:13,400 --> 00:27:17,620
So the first bar,
the yellow bar just

485
00:27:17,620 --> 00:27:20,590
uses the general public data.

486
00:27:20,590 --> 00:27:25,930
And the last bar does have a
50-50 between persona-driven

487
00:27:25,930 --> 00:27:28,610
data and then general data.

488
00:27:28,610 --> 00:27:32,350
And as you see, over
time, we see improvement

489
00:27:32,350 --> 00:27:34,060
mostly on the math.

490
00:27:34,060 --> 00:27:37,670
On GSM8K, we saw
less improvement

491
00:27:37,670 --> 00:27:43,240
and we think the
reason is because GSM8K

492
00:27:43,240 --> 00:27:47,050
are simpler math word problems
like grade school math word

493
00:27:47,050 --> 00:27:47,720
problems.

494
00:27:47,720 --> 00:27:52,450
And we think the available
public data set were already

495
00:27:52,450 --> 00:27:53,090
good enough.

496
00:27:53,090 --> 00:27:55,430
So that's why we
