1126
01:04:55,070 --> 01:05:01,060
And I want to very briefly talk
about test-time inference, which

1127
01:05:01,060 --> 01:05:04,670
is a very popular
topic these days.

1128
01:05:04,670 --> 01:05:07,210
And a lot of people
are looking at how

1129
01:05:07,210 --> 01:05:11,290
to bring in test improvements,
particularly in reasoning

1130
01:05:11,290 --> 01:05:14,020
during test-time inference.

1131
01:05:14,020 --> 01:05:17,980
So I first want to talk
about this paper called

1132
01:05:17,980 --> 01:05:23,410
S1, which is led by
amazing Niklas Muennighoff.

1133
01:05:23,410 --> 01:05:29,140
And it is actually the very
minimal recipe for reasoning

1134
01:05:29,140 --> 01:05:31,450
and test-time scaling.

1135
01:05:31,450 --> 01:05:34,900
So let me tell you how it works.

1136
01:05:34,900 --> 01:05:38,470
Very similar to almost
everything else that

1137
01:05:38,470 --> 01:05:41,500
is happening in
language models, it

1138
01:05:41,500 --> 01:05:45,830
relies on really
carefully curated data.

1139
01:05:45,830 --> 01:05:47,420
We call it s1K.

1140
01:05:47,420 --> 01:05:51,910
Then we apply test-time scaling
algorithm, a very simple one

1141
01:05:51,910 --> 01:05:54,460
and this is the outcome s1.

1142
01:05:54,460 --> 01:05:57,820
OK, how do we curate data?

1143
01:05:57,820 --> 01:06:01,090
We looked at a very
large collection

1144
01:06:01,090 --> 01:06:07,030
of math, logical reasoning,
probability questions, and so

1145
01:06:07,030 --> 01:06:11,120
on, more higher
level reasoning data.

1146
01:06:11,120 --> 01:06:14,860
It's even beyond the Tulu
3 data where we mostly

1147
01:06:14,860 --> 01:06:16,990
looked at grade
school and math, which

1148
01:06:16,990 --> 01:06:20,900
was high school, a little
bit college level math.

1149
01:06:20,900 --> 01:06:25,570
Here we are looking at much
more complex, like Olympiad math

1150
01:06:25,570 --> 01:06:27,910
competition type data.

1151
01:06:27,910 --> 01:06:31,540
So we looked at all these logic
puzzles math and we collected

1152
01:06:31,540 --> 01:06:34,120
59K question.

1153
01:06:34,120 --> 01:06:37,310
But then we did a lot
of filtering here.

1154
01:06:37,310 --> 01:06:39,380
We filtered for quality.

1155
01:06:39,380 --> 01:06:41,660
We got into 52K data.

1156
01:06:41,660 --> 01:06:46,570
We picked the ones that were
more difficult and then we moved

1157
01:06:46,570 --> 01:06:48,620
to 24K.

1158
01:06:48,620 --> 01:06:52,390
And then we emphasized diversity
and we removed the ones

1159
01:06:52,390 --> 01:06:54,140
that they were very similar.

1160
01:06:54,140 --> 01:06:56,060
We ended up having 1K.

1161
01:06:56,060 --> 01:07:01,810
So spoiler alert, on our
benchmarks using 59K data and 1K

1162
01:07:01,810 --> 01:07:06,250
data almost similar,
which is very interesting.

1163
01:07:06,250 --> 01:07:08,870
So once we collected
these prompts,

1164
01:07:08,870 --> 01:07:13,720
we started to distill
reasoning traces and answers.

1165
01:07:13,720 --> 01:07:17,510
So for example, when we
have a problem like this,

1166
01:07:17,510 --> 01:07:21,140
we gave this problem,
which is among our S1 data.

1167
01:07:21,140 --> 01:07:27,760
And then we annotated the
reasoning chain-of-thoughts

1168
01:07:27,760 --> 01:07:31,480
with Google thinking
Gemini model.

1169
01:07:31,480 --> 01:07:35,680
And also we made sure
that this data also

1170
01:07:35,680 --> 01:07:38,980
includes some of the
thinking tokens like, OK,

1171
01:07:38,980 --> 01:07:41,250
this happens, but
let me think more.

1172
01:07:41,250 --> 01:07:41,750
And so on.

1173
01:07:41,750 --> 01:07:45,050
So we use this Google
thinking annotations.

1174
01:07:45,050 --> 01:07:50,140
And most recently in S1, we
replaced Google Gemini result

1175
01:07:50,140 --> 01:07:52,690
annotations with
DeepSeek r1 results

1176
01:07:52,690 --> 01:07:56,710
where it actually improved
the final results, which

1177
01:07:56,710 --> 01:07:59,560
was very interesting to see.

1178
01:07:59,560 --> 01:08:03,430
OK, so this is the final
collection of data.

1179
01:08:03,430 --> 01:08:08,080
The data varies across all these
domains from geometry number

1180
01:08:08,080 --> 01:08:12,810
theory to even various smaller
portion on control theory,

1181
01:08:12,810 --> 01:08:15,230
astronomy, and so on.

1182
01:08:15,230 --> 01:08:18,960
OK, so this is the
data, test-time scaling.

1183
01:08:18,960 --> 01:08:22,460
The simplest method possible,
which made all of us

1184
01:08:22,460 --> 01:08:29,700
very surprised and excited where
we focused on budget forcing,

1185
01:08:29,700 --> 01:08:31,830
where we have a
question like this.

1186
01:08:31,830 --> 01:08:33,450
How many hours are in raspberry?

1187
01:08:33,450 --> 01:08:36,779
We let the model
generate the response.

1188
01:08:36,779 --> 01:08:42,540
But then if it didn't hit
the allocated token budget,

1189
01:08:42,540 --> 01:08:47,359
we added a token called
weight and then let the model

1190
01:08:47,359 --> 01:08:51,529
generate and basically force
the model to generate more

1191
01:08:51,529 --> 01:08:53,040
in order to respond to that.

1192
01:08:53,040 --> 01:08:55,740
We are using this weight token.

1193
01:08:55,740 --> 01:08:58,760
We are hinting the model
that we are not sure

1194
01:08:58,760 --> 01:09:01,664
if your answer is correct.

1195
01:09:01,664 --> 01:09:03,450
So one more thing to add.

1196
01:09:03,450 --> 01:09:06,510
We did not train a model to
decide when to add weight.

1197
01:09:06,510 --> 01:09:12,220
We naturally added weight when
it didn't hit our token model.

1198
01:09:12,220 --> 01:09:13,779
And then we let
the model generate

1199
01:09:13,779 --> 01:09:18,310
more until the final answer
is achieved is obtained.

1200
01:09:18,310 --> 01:09:21,140
OK, so now training and results.

1201
01:09:21,140 --> 01:09:26,300
we trained a Qwen 32B
model on this data.

1202
01:09:26,300 --> 01:09:28,090
And here are our results.

1203
01:09:28,090 --> 01:09:32,710
On the x-axis, we
are seeing the number

1204
01:09:32,710 --> 01:09:34,720
of tokens, the
average thinking time

1205
01:09:34,720 --> 01:09:38,899
or the number of tokens that are
generated and then the accuracy.

1206
01:09:38,899 --> 01:09:44,390
So when we push the model
to just generate 512 tokens,

1207
01:09:44,390 --> 01:09:47,229
here is the accuracy, it's 65.

1208
01:09:47,229 --> 01:09:48,620
One more thing.

1209
01:09:48,620 --> 01:09:54,580
If the model wants to generate
more tokens than the budget,

1210
01:09:54,580 --> 01:09:59,330
we put end of token there
or end of a sentence there.

1211
01:09:59,330 --> 01:10:02,570
So with that, we
control the generation.

1212
01:10:02,570 --> 01:10:09,740
So we go from 5 to
2048 for MATH500 data.

1213
01:10:09,740 --> 01:10:13,580
And as you see over time
with more generated tokens,

1214
01:10:13,580 --> 01:10:16,540
we see this scaling trend.

1215
01:10:16,540 --> 01:10:18,820
On this Matt
competition data, which

1216
01:10:18,820 --> 01:10:22,360
is much more challenging
data, we even

1217
01:10:22,360 --> 01:10:27,700
want the model to generate
longer and longer token outcome.

1218
01:10:27,700 --> 01:10:31,580
And as you see here, we
see, again, the same trend.

1219
01:10:31,580 --> 01:10:34,720
And also PhD level
GPQA demand, again,

1220
01:10:34,720 --> 01:10:38,385
generating longer and longer
tokens, which is very exciting.

1221
01:10:38,385 --> 01:10:41,980

1222
01:10:41,980 --> 01:10:46,150
So zooming in here are a
little bit of how it works.

1223
01:10:46,150 --> 01:10:47,510
I explained it already.

1224
01:10:47,510 --> 01:10:51,830
So when the model wants
to generate more tokens,

1225
01:10:51,830 --> 01:10:56,380
we pushed end of sentence to
be able to get these shorter

1226
01:10:56,380 --> 01:10:58,150
sequences.

1227
01:10:58,150 --> 01:11:02,200
And when we want the
model to generate longer,

1228
01:11:02,200 --> 01:11:07,510
even go beyond 8,000 tokens, we
push this away token and force

1229
01:11:07,510 --> 01:11:10,480
the model to generate more.

1230
01:11:10,480 --> 01:11:16,300
We compared different types
of test-time scaling methods.

1231
01:11:16,300 --> 01:11:19,270
Here are the budget forcing.

1232
01:11:19,270 --> 01:11:23,770
This steeper curve shows the
budget forcing or sequential

1233
01:11:23,770 --> 01:11:24,560
scaling.

1234
01:11:24,560 --> 01:11:30,020
We also did parallel
scaling via majority voting

1235
01:11:30,020 --> 01:11:32,920
where we let the model
generate multiple paths

1236
01:11:32,920 --> 01:11:36,560
and then do majority voting
or the self-consistency check.

1237
01:11:36,560 --> 01:11:40,070
And we saw some gains,
but not that significant.

1238
01:11:40,070 --> 01:11:46,480
So this budget forcing
seemed much more successful

1239
01:11:46,480 --> 01:11:48,280
and much simpler.

1240
01:11:48,280 --> 01:11:50,740
So here are some
of these ablations

1241
01:11:50,740 --> 01:11:53,770
that we have done in the model.

1242
01:11:53,770 --> 01:11:57,250
So this is the final results
on the left-hand side

1243
01:11:57,250 --> 01:12:00,700
of S1K on all those
three data sets.

1244
01:12:00,700 --> 01:12:06,140
Comparing with full collection
of data like using 59K,

1245
01:12:06,140 --> 01:12:11,870
we see that a little bit higher
numbers we get with the full

1246
01:12:11,870 --> 01:12:18,260
data on AIME, but in other
cases pretty much similar.

1247
01:12:18,260 --> 01:12:22,080
And our S1K data is
really high quality.

1248
01:12:22,080 --> 01:12:27,440
If we just use random or just do
a bunch of constraints to find

1249
01:12:27,440 --> 01:12:33,620
that 1,000 data points, the
results are much, much worse.

1250
01:12:33,620 --> 01:12:36,410
We also did a bunch
of scaling ablations

1251
01:12:36,410 --> 01:12:40,580
when we don't increase any--

1252
01:12:40,580 --> 01:12:42,840
let the model to train longer.

1253
01:12:42,840 --> 01:12:43,920
This is the best number.

1254
01:12:43,920 --> 01:12:47,060
So these are the gains
that we are having

1255
01:12:47,060 --> 01:12:49,520
with this test-time scaling.

1256
01:12:49,520 --> 01:12:53,150
If we replace the
weight with other tokens

1257
01:12:53,150 --> 01:12:57,920
alternatively or just let the
model generate without adding

1258
01:12:57,920 --> 01:13:02,570
any strings, we saw less gains.

1259
01:13:02,570 --> 01:13:07,820
OK, so these are our results
for test-time scaling.

1260
01:13:07,820 --> 01:13:12,130
I also want to highlight
two other approaches that we

1261
01:13:12,130 --> 01:13:15,770
have been cooking up over time.

1262
01:13:15,770 --> 01:13:17,330
And the idea is--

1263
01:13:17,330 --> 01:13:20,050
and I'm not going to talk about
any details on these next two

1264
01:13:20,050 --> 01:13:24,640
methods, but the idea is
we want to be very aware

1265
01:13:24,640 --> 01:13:28,370
and do self-guided
generation at inference time.

1266
01:13:28,370 --> 01:13:34,600
In this work self-reg, we build
a rack-based model retrieval

1267
01:13:34,600 --> 01:13:36,710
augmented generation model.

1268
01:13:36,710 --> 01:13:40,780
But in this setting, we
trained a language model

1269
01:13:40,780 --> 01:13:44,950
to generate tokens, but
then criticize its outcomes

1270
01:13:44,950 --> 01:13:46,370
and self-improve.

1271
01:13:46,370 --> 01:13:50,830
And we did it to the
whole loop with generating

1272
01:13:50,830 --> 01:13:55,910
these critic tokens where the
model generates responses,

1273
01:13:55,910 --> 01:13:58,570
but once in a while,
it generates tokens

1274
01:13:58,570 --> 01:14:03,290
to say if the generated
response makes sense

1275
01:14:03,290 --> 01:14:07,140
or not or if they retrieve
documents make sense or not.

1276
01:14:07,140 --> 01:14:10,370
So these are also very
promising directions

1277
01:14:10,370 --> 01:14:13,280
in pushing for
test-time scaling.

1278
01:14:13,280 --> 01:14:19,400
We also showed that how
this self-guided improvement

1279
01:14:19,400 --> 01:14:23,540
loop helps in synthesizing
scientific literature

1280
01:14:23,540 --> 01:14:28,610
and answering very complex
questions about scientific tasks

1281
01:14:28,610 --> 01:14:30,960
that require lots and
lots of reasoning.

1282
01:14:30,960 --> 01:14:33,400
So please check out
these two papers.

1283
01:14:33,400 --> 01:14:36,320
The results are
very interesting.

1284
01:14:36,320 --> 01:14:42,350
We also have a demo, which is
openscholar.allen.ai, where

1285
01:14:42,350 --> 01:14:46,790
you can play with this
system and type your question

1286
01:14:46,790 --> 01:14:49,910
and then see how it
can answer and combine

1287
01:14:49,910 --> 01:14:53,180
different pieces of information,
synthesize knowledge, and then

1288
01:14:53,180 --> 01:14:57,260
answer queries for
this type of task.

1289
01:14:57,260 --> 01:15:03,620
OK, this is a demo and I
would let you play with it.

1290
01:15:03,620 --> 01:15:07,970
So I want to very
briefly also talk

1291
01:15:07,970 --> 01:15:14,100
about how reasoning can get
integrated during pretraining.

1292
01:15:14,100 --> 01:15:19,610
OK, so far what I talked about
was post-training integrating

1293
01:15:19,610 --> 01:15:21,540
things at inference time.

1294
01:15:21,540 --> 01:15:24,350
And now, let's go
backward and then say

1295
01:15:24,350 --> 01:15:28,170
see how these things can
be added during training.

1296
01:15:28,170 --> 01:15:31,040
Remember, when I
said RLVR didn't work

1297
01:15:31,040 --> 01:15:33,180
when we had weaker base model.

1298
01:15:33,180 --> 01:15:36,950
So we need to push to have
stronger and stronger base

1299
01:15:36,950 --> 01:15:39,081
models.

1300
01:15:39,081 --> 01:15:41,150
So this is of--

1301
01:15:41,150 --> 01:15:43,440
when we say
training-based model,

1302
01:15:43,440 --> 01:15:46,460
it's not just training with
the next token prediction

1303
01:15:46,460 --> 01:15:49,410
where learning rate
stays the same and so on.

1304
01:15:49,410 --> 01:15:52,800
We usually do multiple
stages of training.

1305
01:15:52,800 --> 01:15:57,860
In this first stage we
have the warm period, which

1306
01:15:57,860 --> 01:15:59,850
is a very standard method.

1307
01:15:59,850 --> 01:16:03,620
But then we use
cosine learning rate

1308
01:16:03,620 --> 01:16:06,710
to train these models with
the next token prediction

1309
01:16:06,710 --> 01:16:09,172
on trillions of tokens.

1310
01:16:09,172 --> 01:16:13,950
But then toward the end of
training, we curate really,

1311
01:16:13,950 --> 01:16:17,060
really high quality
data in a lot of cases

1312
01:16:17,060 --> 01:16:19,940
designed to do more
complex reasoning.

1313
01:16:19,940 --> 01:16:22,520
And then we take
the model that is

1314
01:16:22,520 --> 01:16:28,520
here, the learning rate that is
here, and then a nearly to 0.

1315
01:16:28,520 --> 01:16:32,510
Sometimes this stage
is called mid-training.

1316
01:16:32,510 --> 01:16:35,690
The role of these
50 billion tokens

1317
01:16:35,690 --> 01:16:39,930
is very important because we
still do next token prediction.

1318
01:16:39,930 --> 01:16:42,350
But these tokens are very--

1319
01:16:42,350 --> 01:16:46,250
are trying to focus
on very complex tasks

1320
01:16:46,250 --> 01:16:51,860
and emphasize on more
reasoning-oriented setting.

1321
01:16:51,860 --> 01:16:55,940
And roughly this is called
nowadays pretraining

1322
01:16:55,940 --> 01:16:58,640
and then mid-training.

1323
01:16:58,640 --> 01:17:02,030
So the pretraining
stage, it uses

1324
01:17:02,030 --> 01:17:05,420
most of the budget in
the training stage.

1325
01:17:05,420 --> 01:17:07,620
It uses trillions of tokens.

1326
01:17:07,620 --> 01:17:11,130
It's pretty much
unstructured, diverse text.

1327
01:17:11,130 --> 01:17:14,330
Lots of it comes from web data.

1328
01:17:14,330 --> 01:17:16,910
And we try to use
the best type of data

1329
01:17:16,910 --> 01:17:20,240
that we could collect given the
compute budget that we have.

1330
01:17:20,240 --> 01:17:24,380
During mid-training, it's
almost 1% of training.

1331
01:17:24,380 --> 01:17:27,560
The idea is we're going
to upsample high quality

1332
01:17:27,560 --> 01:17:30,020
data that we have
during pretraining

1333
01:17:30,020 --> 01:17:33,750
and then to make sure that
we have some in-domain data.

1334
01:17:33,750 --> 01:17:37,370
But then we make sure to bring
some event supervised fine

1335
01:17:37,370 --> 01:17:40,610
tuning data, we bring in a
lot of reasoning type data

1336
01:17:40,610 --> 01:17:42,440
coding data.

1337
01:17:42,440 --> 01:17:47,730
And we try to emphasize the
quality of the data here.

1338
01:17:47,730 --> 01:17:50,510
So this is for example,
the pretraining data mix

1339
01:17:50,510 --> 01:17:55,350
that we use for OLMo 2, which
are some of the web pages.

1340
01:17:55,350 --> 01:17:56,550
Some code data.

1341
01:17:56,550 --> 01:17:59,790
I mean, the leftmost
column matters less,

1342
01:17:59,790 --> 01:18:01,970
the rightmost categories
make more sense

1343
01:18:01,970 --> 01:18:05,820
to follow right now, our
web pages, code data,

1344
01:18:05,820 --> 01:18:09,890
academic papers, a stem papers,
some math web pages, and math

1345
01:18:09,890 --> 01:18:10,920
proof, and so on.

1346
01:18:10,920 --> 01:18:15,500
And in total we have
roughly 4 trillion tokens.

1347
01:18:15,500 --> 01:18:18,960
But then during
mid-training stage,

1348
01:18:18,960 --> 01:18:24,080
we started to bring in lots
and lots of math data, which

1349
01:18:24,080 --> 01:18:26,630
started to patch
the math reasoning

1350
01:18:26,630 --> 01:18:29,460
capabilities that the model
didn't originally have.

1351
01:18:29,460 --> 01:18:32,310
For example, this was very
interesting observation.

1352
01:18:32,310 --> 01:18:35,210
We realized at the
end of this stage

1353
01:18:35,210 --> 01:18:39,120
the OLMo model was not that
good in doing multiplication,

1354
01:18:39,120 --> 01:18:41,880
but much better in doing
addition and subtraction.

1355
01:18:41,880 --> 01:18:49,010
So we started integrating some
synthetic multiplication data

1356
01:18:49,010 --> 01:18:51,265
in the mid-training set.

1357
01:18:51,265 --> 01:18:55,640
So we you cannot observe do
a lot of evaluations and then

1358
01:18:55,640 --> 01:18:58,280
figure out, do some patching
to improve the model.

1359
01:18:58,280 --> 01:19:02,600
And then inject that type
of knowledge in the model.

1360
01:19:02,600 --> 01:19:04,920
So as you see in our
mid-training mix,

1361
01:19:04,920 --> 01:19:11,870
we included a lot of
reasoning type data.

1362
01:19:11,870 --> 01:19:15,030
And then we started
evaluating these models.

1363
01:19:15,030 --> 01:19:21,170
So this is evaluating our
pretraining and mid-training

1364
01:19:21,170 --> 01:19:21,870
together.

1365
01:19:21,870 --> 01:19:24,530
On average, we see
significant boost

1366
01:19:24,530 --> 01:19:27,540
in both setting like
from 50 we go to 60.

1367
01:19:27,540 --> 01:19:29,940
From 56 we go to 68.

1368
01:19:29,940 --> 01:19:34,520
And the biggest
gains are on GSM8K

1369
01:19:34,520 --> 01:19:37,730
which is our math reasoning
capabilities and drop, which

1370
01:19:37,730 --> 01:19:41,180
is also question answering
with math data, which

1371
01:19:41,180 --> 01:19:43,050
is showing significant
boosts there,

1372
01:19:43,050 --> 01:19:46,070
which is very interesting.

1373
01:19:46,070 --> 01:19:49,380
OK, so putting all
these together,

1374
01:19:49,380 --> 01:19:55,040
here are our two results, which
is on par with Llama 3 8B.

1375
01:19:55,040 --> 01:19:59,490
Please refer to the
paper to look at the 13B.

1376
01:19:59,490 --> 01:20:03,290
And most recently, we're
going to have a 32B results.

1377
01:20:03,290 --> 01:20:07,160
OK, so with that, I
want to finish this

1378
01:20:07,160 --> 01:20:12,560
that we've seen a lot of
progress in today's AI work.

1379
01:20:12,560 --> 01:20:15,470
Still we need a lot of
research and innovation

1380
01:20:15,470 --> 01:20:17,430
in many newer tasks.

1381
01:20:17,430 --> 01:20:19,790
So here I've listed
a lot of them,

1382
01:20:19,790 --> 01:20:23,000
a lot of them relevant
to reasoning agents,

1383
01:20:23,000 --> 01:20:25,700
language models
for other domains,

1384
01:20:25,700 --> 01:20:28,730
and so on, which I
really would be excited

1385
